WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.891
Film Courage:&nbsp; This question comes in from
Stian Kallhovd (forgive me for saying the

00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:09.440
name incorrectly)&nbsp; “I’ve sometimes wondered
whether I should write TV series in addition

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.470
to features, but so far it seems to me that
the stories I want to tell are better told

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:19.600
through films. Can Russell give an example
of why a story would be better told as a TV

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.600
series?

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.510
Peter Russell, screenwriter/story doctor:
Well if you love movie and so many people

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:30.599
do I know a lot of people who grew up loving
movies are kind of appalled that television

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:33.000
has taken over.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.050
Think of it this way you can tell any movie
as a TV miniseries. You can take the movie

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:47.500
that you’ve been writing over the last four
or five years and you can turn it into a television

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:55.600
miniseries more easily than you can turn it
into a TV series. So think of it that way.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:05.469
Now I spent several years learning genres:
mystery thriller, crime thriller, horror,

00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:12.560
all of these genres and I sort of master the
deep understanding of each one of those genres

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.229
and every one of those genres is very different
and they all have these cool tools that I

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:25.509
learned to help movie people. Well guess what?
It turns out when I turn my attention to television,

00:01:25.509 --> 00:01:31.479
all the rules of movie thrillers apply to
the crime drama that I’m selling as a serial

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:37.719
right now Ray Donovan meets True Detective.
All of those cool structural tools that work

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:44.479
in SEVEN, that work in all these classic movies,
it turns out those secrets transfer right

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.990
over to television.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:54.530
So just know that everything you’ve learned
about movies works in TV. But the easiest

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:01.740
transition is to take your movie idea and
turn it into a miniseries. Why? Which for

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:08.209
some of the reasons we talked about earlier
which is miniseries are like long movies because

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:13.959
the characters have an arc. They start with
a wound and they eventually heal or not.

00:02:13.959 --> 00:02:20.349
So if you’re writing movies and you love
movies, make the transition to television

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:28.540
by writing a miniseries, where you can simply
stretch your idea into a longer form. Forget

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:34.680
the structure so much, but those cool tools
which by the way I have on my website Peter

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:41.680
Russell Script Doctor (dot) com. I go each
genre of movies has a set of hidden structures

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:47.990
and tools in it. Just take those hidden structures
and tools and apply them to your television

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.460
show in the same genre.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:57.590
Writing a comedy? Apply the movie comedic
rules to your sitcom. Writing a romantic comedy,

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:05.480
same thing. Love story, same thing. Adventure
romance, same thing. Write a miniseries, it’s

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:12.670
a bridge form. It will help you learn why
television has two big differences than movies.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:18.400
One is that the characters don’t really
change in television and the other is you

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.360
can tell more than you can in a movie where
you want to show everything (so there’s

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.400
just more dialogue).

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.470
But those are the two big differences, everything
else can be the same. Especially in a miniseries.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:39.960
It’s a great bridge for you to learn this
new structure. And you’ll just have a better

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:47.680
chance of getting your story bought. I didn’t
say great chance! I said better chance because

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:53.280
I think we’re up to 550 scripted television
shows now in the U.S. A few years ago there

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:00.790
were 200. And I believe that’s going to
continue. I think in 10 years television is

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.560
going to be five times as big as it is right
now.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:11.450
So if you want your work to sell learn the
new forms by bridging your way to TV by using

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.599
your movie as a miniseries.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:19.220
Film Courage: We miniseries in fashion so-to-speak
previously?

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:25.600
Peter: They were in the 70’s. I know there
were big miniseries in the 70’s like the

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:33.919
THORN BIRDS. There was another show called
SHOGUN. These were big event television so

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:38.960
they’d be on one night for three or four
nights, five nights sometimes. But then there

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.790
was a period where they weren’t fashionable.
But they’ve gotten very fashionable again.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:53.430
Ryan Murphy, all these guys that write these
fantastic shows. The OJ Simpson trial [THE

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:58.160
PEOPLE V. O.J. SIMPSON]. TRUST which is a
great one that Danny Boyle just did about

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.970
the Getty family, brilliant miniseries.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:12.160
And THE ASSASSINATION OF GIANI VERSACE they
are where I think a lot of movie makers are

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:19.860
going in television because it’s a more
commodious form, it’s the easiest way to

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.699
transition from film to television.

00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:32.220
And I think the miniseries also kind of satisfies
our desire for seeing a movie which is I want

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:38.240
to see somebody change, I want to see a complete
story right? When you’re watching ANIMAL

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.460
KINGDOM, well okay Ellen Barkin is still going
to be a poisonous b*tch, okay, well let’s

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:50.211
have some popcorn. At some point you want
to see what’s going to happen right? Can

00:05:50.211 --> 00:05:57.080
we have some resolution, can we actually resolve
something. That’s what great movies have

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:04.360
always done for us, so I think that instinct
to have a complete story, that’s why miniseries

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:09.139
have become so much more popular. They’re
sort of replacing what movies used to give

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:12.069
us which is the sense of a complete story.

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:19.270
“Ahh, we’re done. Ellen Barkin is. Yeah,
two of the kids got help. They got therapy.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:23.680
Thank God we can have dinner now.” As opposed
to “Oh God she’s still in jail, she’s

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.371
really tormented right. Is she still wearing
those awful black contacts, yes she is. She

00:06:28.371 --> 00:06:30.560
still looks like, right?

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:36.570
In the movie, she would have moved on. She’d
probably be or should be okay. But that’s

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.740
why miniseries I think are so popular.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:44.129
Film Courage: You said in the 70’s [miniseries]
were popular. Can you see any parallels between

00:06:44.129 --> 00:06:47.330
that time and now? Is there something in audiences
that wants this?

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:56.650
Peter: Yes, I think that we love long form
entertainment. And that’s why I think we

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:02.470
binge series, too. I think entertainment is
becoming like the Russian novel of the 19th

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:07.419
century. What do I mean by this? It sounds
so pretentious, I’m sorry. But the Russian

00:07:07.419 --> 00:07:13.460
novel (Tolstoy) got longer and longer and
bigger and bigger, until they were 1,000 pages,

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.110
1,500 pages, right?

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:20.770
That’s the sign of a maturity of an art
form. That’s when an art form gets its best,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:25.340
when it gets big, when it gets long. I think
we’re in that we’re in that (Suzy hates

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:30.150
me saying The Golden Age) but I think we are
in a Golden Age of Entertainment because the

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.400
stories are getting so much longer. Look,
you can tell everybody is neither a good guy

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.830
or a bad guy. Everybody is at a combination
of traits in them.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.240
So a movie can’t really show you that, there’s
not enough time. You’re either good or you’re

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.800
bad. Television and the longer form can show
both the good and the bad inside of all of

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.349
us. It’s a more sophisticated model of human
behavior. I think we’re just getting to

00:07:55.349 --> 00:08:02.520
be more sophisticated as people to know that
people’s stories are complex. I’m not

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:08.580
all good. I’m not all evil. I’m mostly
evil but there’s some good in me but that’s

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:16.021
the mix. And so longer form art can give us
a more complete picture of what humanity is

00:08:16.021 --> 00:08:16.030
about.

